    @section jni_3_0_0 jni Module Version 3.0.0

    - <b>Security</b>: %Qore code that Java calls on a Java thread - a
      @ref Jni::org::qore::jni::QoreInvocationHandler "QoreInvocationHandler" proxy, a closure passed to Java, or a
      method of a %Qore object passed to Java - now runs under the sandbox of the code that handed it to Java, and is
      released or destroyed under it; previously it ran with no sandbox on a Java thread that %Qore did not start
      (see @ref jni_callback_sandbox)
    - fixed a crash when a %Qore callback on a Java thread made a Java call that called back into %Qore on the same
      thread: the inner callback detached the thread from %Qore while the outer one was still running
    - fixed a data race when a child Program was created while another thread imported a Java class into its
      parent: the child copied the parent's class map without the lock that protects it
    - fixed a Java method call ending the process when its thread could not be attached to the JVM, as when the
      thread's cancellation was pending; the call now raises the exception (ex: \c THREAD-CANCELLED)
    - fixed a child process created with \c fork() after the JVM was started waiting forever when it exited or ended
      its script, or when it deleted a Program whose %Qore code a Java thread was running; Java cannot be used in
      such a child, and a use of Java there now raises \c JNI-FORK-ERROR (see @ref jni_fork)
    - fixed converting a list of binary values, closures or call references to Java where the Java type is not
      given (for example for a Java \c Object parameter, or for a %Qore class constant in the Java class generated
      for the class): such a list now becomes an array of byte arrays (\c byte[][]) or of
      \c org.qore.jni.QoreClosure objects; the conversion raised \c JNI-ERROR ("cannot create a Java array from a
      list that contains values of type 'binary'"), and defining the Java class of a %Qore class with such a
      constant failed with \c java.lang.ExceptionInInitializerError
    - fixed deep recursion on the primary thread after the JVM was created overflowing the stack instead of raising
      \c STACK-LIMIT-EXCEEDED: the primary thread's stack limit was set for the default thread stack size, from the
      position of the stack when %Qore started, without the primary thread's extra stack guard and without the zones
      that the JVM protects at the end of the stack; it is now set to the end of the stack as the JVM sees it (see
      @ref jni_primary_thread_stack)
    - made Java socket-based JDBC connection establishment cancellable using a joined virtual thread
    - fixed a native abort while converting a Java exception with Qore cancellation pending
    - closed connections after failed metadata/session initialization and cancellation races


