    @subsection jni_3_0_0_overview Overview

    JNI 3.0 brings Kotlin language integration, new data providers, columnar JDBC results, and improved
    Java API generation to %Qore 3.0. It requires <b>%Qore 3.0 and Java 21 or later</b>.

    When upgrading:
    - <b>Update Java imports for module APIs.</b> Module-owned classes now use only
      <tt>qoremod.&lt;module&gt;.&lt;class-path&gt;</tt>; the legacy
      <tt>qore.&lt;module&gt;.&lt;class-path&gt;</tt> aliases are no longer accepted. Recompile affected Java
      sources and regenerate API classes created by older JNI versions. See
      @ref jni_dynamic_import_migration "Java import migration" for examples and the
      \c qjava-migrate-imports helper.
    - <b>Review collection types at the language boundary.</b> Generated Java signatures for
      <tt>hash&lt;auto&gt;</tt> parameters now use \c java.util.Map. Java and Kotlin collections convert
      to the most specific %Qore list or hash type, which can restrict later insertion of values with
      other types. See @ref jni_java_to_qore "Java to Qore type conversions".
    - <b>Review code using Java lists and maps as Java objects.</b> The %Qore types declared for Java methods and
      fields now match the values they return: every Java list, such as \c ArrayList or \c LinkedList, is declared
      as <tt>*list&lt;auto&gt;</tt>, and a Java map as <tt>*hash&lt;auto&gt;</tt> only when its key type is \c String; any other map is
      declared as \c auto, as it is a hash or a Java object depending on its keys (a method returning
      <tt>TreeMap&lt;Integer, String&gt;</tt> still returns a Java object, and is now declared as \c auto).  Code
      that assigns a returned \c ArrayList to a variable declared as the Java class, or that overrides such a method
      with a Java object return type, now fails when it is parsed; use a list instead.  A raw \c java.util.Map return
      value, declared as <tt>*hash&lt;auto&gt;</tt> before, is declared as \c auto; this only changes parse-time type checks.
      See @ref jni_collection_types "declared types of Java lists and maps".
    - <b>Review code using Java date and time values.</b> The values of \c java.time.OffsetDateTime,
      \c java.time.OffsetTime, \c java.time.LocalDate and \c java.time.LocalTime are now %Qore dates, as those of the
      other Java date and time classes were; code calling Java methods on them must use %Qore date operations
      instead.  The members of the Java classes whose values are dates, also \c java.time.LocalDateTime,
      \c java.time.Instant, \c java.sql.Timestamp, \c java.sql.Date and \c java.sql.Time, are declared with the
      \c date type: a method of one of these types returned a date already but was declared with the Java class, so
      that a variable declared with the Java class for its value no longer parses (it could only hold \c NOTHING);
      a parameter of one of them takes a date, as well as the Java object.  See
      @ref jni_date_time_classes "Java date and time classes".
    - <b>Recompile ahead-of-time compiled code with %Qore classes that inherit Java classes.</b> The types of
      private Java members are now declared as \c auto (see @ref jni_member_types "declared types of Java members"),
      and the members of the Java date and time classes as dates (see
      @ref jni_date_time_classes "Java date and time classes"), which changes the signatures of the %Qore classes
      created for Java classes, and a %Qore class's signature includes those of its parent classes.  Recompile code
      compiled with \c qcc that defines %Qore classes inheriting Java classes with this version; otherwise its
      classes do not match the same classes parsed from source.
    - <b>Review code using the keys of duplicate columns in \c jdbc results.</b> A duplicate column name is the
      lower-case name with a suffix, as for module-odbc and module-sybase: the columns \c ID and \c ID of a join are
      returned as \c id and \c id_1, and no longer as \c id and \c ID_1 (the JDBC drivers of Oracle and SQL Server
      return the names as written, often in upper case).
    - <b>Update handlers of the \c jdbc driver's lost-transaction error.</b> When the connection is lost in a
      transaction, the \c jdbc DBI driver raises \c JDBC-TRANSACTION-ERROR; it raised \c ODBC-TRANSACTION-ERROR
      before.  Code that catches the old error code must catch the new one.  See
      @ref jdbc_lost_connections "lost connections".
    - <b>Review code executing SQL with several results through the \c jdbc driver.</b> \c exec(),
      \c execRaw() and \c select() return every result of such SQL, and the shape of the value depends on the
      results; \c selectRow(), \c selectRows(), \c selectColumnar() and \c SQLStatement raise an exception
      for more than one result set.  See @ref jdbc_multiple_results "statements with several results".
    - <b>Use explicit lifecycle APIs where deterministic cleanup matters.</b> Native wrappers use
      automatic native-reference cleanup instead of Java finalizers; explicit release remains available. See
      @ref jni_qore_object_lifecycle_managementguide "object lifecycle management" and
      @ref org.qore.jni.QoreObjectBase.release().

    @subsection jni_3_0_0_features New Features

    @subsubsection jni_3_0_0_kotlin Kotlin Integration

    - Compile Kotlin code that uses %Qore APIs with \c qkotlinc, and use the \c kotlin.* and
      \c kotlinmod.* dynamic import prefixes. Kotlin synthetic members, bridge methods, default-parameter
      variants, and companion-object classes are filtered from imported APIs. See the
      @ref jni_kotlin_example "Kotlin example" and @ref jni_dynamic_imports_compile "compilation guide".
    - Evaluate Kotlin scripts at runtime with @ref Jni::org::qore::jni::kotlin_eval() "kotlin_eval()",
      or at parse time with the \c kotlin-eval module command. Scripting requires the Kotlin scripting
      JARs, which can be fetched with \c download-kotlin-scripting-jars. Check availability with
      @ref Jni::org::qore::jni::kotlin_scripting_available() "kotlin_scripting_available()" and retry
      initialization after adding JARs with
      @ref Jni::org::qore::jni::kotlin_scripting_retry() "kotlin_scripting_retry()".
    - Kotlin and Java maps and lists participate in automatic %Qore collection conversion.
      \c java.time.LocalDateTime and \c java.time.Instant also convert to %Qore dates, preserving local
      date-time and UTC semantics respectively. See @ref jni_typesguide "type conversions".

    @subsubsection jni_3_0_0_providers Data Provider Integrations

    - <b>Messaging and industrial connectivity:</b>
      @ref jakartajmsdataproviderintro "JakartaJmsDataProvider" adds Jakarta JMS 3.x messaging, including
      send, request/reply, queue browsing, event-driven consumption, selectors, and durable subscriptions.
      @ref opcuadataproviderintro "OpcUaDataProvider" adds OPC UA browsing, reads, writes, method calls,
      subscriptions, security configuration, and offline NodeSet2 import/export.
    - <b>Database and application integration:</b>
      @ref jdbcdataproviderintro "JdbcDataProvider" adds database access and schema introspection with
      configurable driver JARs; @ref flywaydataproviderintro "FlywayDataProvider" manages database
      migrations; @ref cameldataproviderintro "CamelDataProvider" exposes Apache Camel integrations;
      @ref accessdataproviderintro "AccessDataProvider" reads Microsoft Access databases.
    - <b>Documents and reports:</b> new providers support
      @ref avrodataproviderintro "Avro", @ref odsdataproviderintro "ODS",
      @ref odtdataproviderintro "ODT", @ref odpdataproviderintro "ODP",
      @ref worddataproviderintro "Word", @ref powerpointdataproviderintro "PowerPoint",
      @ref vcfdataproviderintro "vCard", @ref icsdataproviderintro "iCalendar", and
      @ref emaildataproviderintro "email" formats. Document-writing actions can return generated data
      or write a file, including new writing actions in @ref exceldataproviderintro "ExcelDataProvider".
      @ref jasperreportsdataproviderintro "JasperReportsDataProvider" generates reports;
      @ref tikadataproviderintro "TikaDataProvider" extracts content, and
      @ref visiodataproviderintro "VisioDataProvider" reads Visio documents. Document-writing actions
      require DataProvider 3.9 or later.
    - <b>Existing provider enhancements:</b> @ref kafkadataproviderintro "KafkaDataProvider" supports
      record headers and producer requests without keys. @ref exceldataproviderintro "ExcelDataProvider"
      and @ref odsdataproviderintro "OdsDataProvider" add the \c ignore_empty read option to skip empty rows,
      and read records one row at a time from a temporary copy of their input, so .xlsx and ODS sheets with
      millions of rows are read in bounded memory instead of loading the whole workbook. Their writers add the
      \c column_formats option, a display format code for each column (such as \c dd.mm.yyyy or
      <tt>#,##0.00</tt>), so the cells keep their dates and numbers and are shown as intended in Excel and
      LibreOffice, and the \c column_masks option, the display format of each column as a mask of %Qore's
      \c ValueMask module (such as \c DD.MM.YYYY or <tt>#.##0,00 €</tt>), the masks that CSV files and Qorus
      sheet contracts are read and written with; the \c column_headers option gives header text that is not the
      column name, \c write_headers set to \c false writes no header row, and the \c timezone option gives the
      time zone whose wall-clock time dates are written in.
    - <b>Behavior change: Excel cells now hold a date's own wall-clock time, as ODS cells do.</b> The
      @ref exceldataproviderintro "ExcelDataProvider" writer wrote a date as the wall-clock time of the Java
      virtual machine's time zone.  This affects files written by development builds of the writer, which is new
      in this release, from dates in another time zone than the Java virtual machine's (for example a date with a
      UTC offset from an API, written by a server in another time zone); dates in the server's own time zone are
      written as before.  Use the \c timezone option to write dates in a given time zone.

    @subsubsection jni_3_0_0_columnar Columnar JDBC Results

    - The @ref jnijdbc_driverguide "jdbc DBI driver" supports columnar queries and fetches, with dense
      nullable buffers for supported column types and list storage for driver-specific values.
      Packed decimal and temporal columns retain their precision and schema metadata. Batch extraction
      reduces per-cell JNI calls; see @ref jdbc_columnar_results "columnar result support" for the
      supported types and storage rules.
    - Java callers can use @ref org.qore.lang.AbstractDatasource.selectColumnar(),
      @ref org.qore.lang.AbstractDatasource.vselectColumnar(), and
      @ref org.qore.lang.AbstractSQLStatement.fetchColumnar().

    @subsubsection jni_3_0_0_java_api Java API Generation and Interoperability

    - Generated Java APIs preserve %Qore generic parent, interface, return, and parameter types when
      the %Qore generic-class API is available. See @ref jni_dynamic_import_generics "generic signatures".
    - Module-owned classes share one Java class identity across consuming Programs. Applications can
      also opt a non-module API class into shared identity with \c register-shared-class before loading
      it in sibling Program classloaders. See @ref jni_classloader "classloader identity and registration".
    - A %Qore method overriding or implementing a Java method is generated with the Java method's signature, and
      its return value is converted to the Java method's return type, so that Java calls it for methods with list,
      map, or numeric types that differ from the %Qore types (for example a Java \c ArrayList or \c int return
      type). See @ref jni_collection_overrides.
    - The Java method called for overloads with the same %Qore signature is chosen deterministically, as Java
      would choose it for the Java object that a %Qore list or hash is converted to; a Java object argument is
      passed to the method of its class rather than of a superclass or interface. See
      @ref jni_collection_overloads.
    - A Java class that cannot be loaded for a member's signature is declared as \c auto and reported once per class
      with the underlying Java exception, only for members that %Qore code can call (public, protected and
      package-private members).  The types of private members are declared as \c auto without importing the
      classes they name, so a library whose optional dependency is used only internally is imported without a
      report (the SQL Server JDBC driver without antlr no longer reports \c SQLServerFMTQuery), and importing a
      class creates fewer %Qore classes.  See @ref jni_member_types "declared types of Java members".
    - The bundled Byte Buddy library is updated from 1.14.19 to 1.18.10, adding support for newer Java
      class-file versions and bytecode-based superclass descriptions.

    @subsubsection jni_3_0_0_runtime Runtime and Resource Management

    - Added interruptible I/O support so sandboxed programs can terminate gracefully, with interrupt
      checks before JVM thread attachment; see @ref sandbox_interrupt "safe code interruption".
    - Native access for unnamed modules is enabled by default when JNI starts the JVM. The
      \c QORE_JNI_DISABLE_NATIVE_ACCESS environment variable disables this option, and
      @ref Jni::org::qore::jni::native_access_option_enabled() "native_access_option_enabled()" reports
      its status. See @ref jniinitguide "JVM initialization".
    - Native wrappers support automatic cleanup and safe repeated explicit release;
      see @ref org.qore.jni.QoreObjectBase.release() and @ref org.qore.jni.QoreObjectBase.destroy().
      @ref org.qore.jni.compiler.QoreJavaCompiler now implements \c AutoCloseable for use with Java
      try-with-resources; see @ref org.qore.jni.compiler.QoreJavaCompiler.close().
    - @ref org.qore.jni.QoreURLClassLoader.getValidPtr() validates the backing Program;
      @ref org.qore.jni.QoreURLClassLoader.clearContext() and
      @ref org.qore.jni.QoreURLClassLoader.clearAllCaches() provide explicit context and cache cleanup.
    - Java provider dependency profiles validate JAR checksums, classpath contents, service entries,
      logging-provider compatibility, and logging bridge cycles in source, AOT, and installed builds.

    @subsection jni_3_0_0_bugfixes Bugfixes

    @par Java imports and class generation

    - Fixed deadlocks during concurrent Java imports, module loading, and dynamic bytecode generation.
      Fixed incorrect binding to a same-named class in another Program, missing classes from private
      module dependencies, mutually dependent class-generation failures, and inherited module-class
      lookup. See @ref jni_dynamic_import_in_java and @ref jni_classloader.
    - Fixed callback arguments resolving to the wrong %Qore class and callbacks failing when invoked
      through module method references. Callback dispatch now retains the owning Program context;
      see @ref Jni::org::qore::jni::implement_interface() "implement_interface()".
    - Fixed abstract-method resolution for Java classes imported through Python, fallback classpath
      handling when the parent loader is not a %Qore classloader, and cache invalidation in
      @ref org.qore.jni.QoreURLClassLoader.clearCompilationCache().
    - Fixed Java compiler diagnostics being written only to standard error instead of being included
      in the exception; see @ref org.qore.jni.compiler.QoreJavaCompiler.
    - Fixed a %Qore class implementing a Java interface imported from the JDK or a JAR failing when its Java class
      was generated, because \c java.lang.Object was taken as its Java base class, or because a class was generated
      for the interface itself instead of using it (for nested interfaces and JDK packages).

    @par Type conversions and fields

    - Fixed Java \c java.time.OffsetDateTime, \c java.time.OffsetTime, \c java.time.LocalDate and
      \c java.time.LocalTime values being returned as Java objects instead of dates, for example the
      <tt>TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE</tt> values of the Firebird JDBC driver and the \c java.time values that JDBC 4.2
      drivers return: an \c OffsetDateTime keeps its offset, and a time of day is a time on \c 1970-01-01, as for a
      \c java.sql.Time.  The members of the Java date and time classes are declared as dates, and a date passed for
      a parameter or field of one of them is converted to its class; before, a date could only be passed for a
      \c java.time.ZonedDateTime.  The \c jdbc driver describes such columns as dates.  See
      @ref jni_date_time_classes "Java date and time classes".

    - Mutable Java static fields now stay synchronized with their %Qore static-variable mappings.
      Map and list fields accept both the Java object and its compatible %Qore representation;
      see @ref jni_class_fields "Java field mappings".
    - Fixed the JVM crashing when a callback implementing a Java interface method with
      @ref Jni::org::qore::jni::QoreInvocationHandler "QoreInvocationHandler" returned a hash.
    - A %Qore list or hash given for a list or map parameter whose class has no public constructor without
      arguments raises an exception instead of passing an object of another class to Java.
    - Fixed %Qore lists being rejected for Java collection parameters and fields. Empty lists remain
      empty arrays rather than becoming \c null, and empty trailing varargs are accepted in Debug
      builds. See @ref jni_typesguide and @ref jni_array_varargs.
    - Fixed duplicate JNI reference deletion in array construction and crashes during recursive
      class generation. Java exception conversion preserves the original exception if reading its
      metadata fails, and Java native stack frames no longer abort Debug %Qore;
      see @ref jni_exceptions "Java exceptions in Qore".
    - A \c java.time.ZonedDateTime at a whole minute keeps its time zone when converted to a %Qore date;
      before, it was read in the local time zone. This also corrects the date and time cells of the
      @ref exceldataproviderintro "Excel" and @ref odsdataproviderintro "ODS" readers with the
      \c timezone option, which are now the clock time written in the file, in that zone.

    @par Method invocation

    - Fixed \c IllegalAccessException when calling a public method of an object whose implementation class
      is not public and is in a closed module, such as \c size() on the unmodifiable set of a Kafka
      consumer's partition assignment; the method is now invoked through its accessible public interface or
      superclass declaration, without \c --add-opens. Virtual dispatch, private access restrictions, target
      exceptions, and the context class loader are preserved, and the declaration is resolved once per
      method.
    - Fixed \c INVALD-STACK-FRAME errors when %Qore code called from Java reads the frames of its call stack
      one at a time, as \c QUnit does when a Java program creates a test object: a read that stopped at a
      Java frame left the next read with fewer frames.

    - Fixed the process crashing (an assertion failing in Debug builds) when a thread's first call into Java was
      @ref Jni::org::qore::jni::load_class() "load_class()" for a class already imported, or
      @ref Jni::org::qore::jni::new_array() "new_array()": neither attached the thread to the JVM.

    @par Lifetimes and shutdown

    - Fixed crashes from expired Program bindings, callback metadata, and incomplete Java wrapper
      construction. Generated API classes reject stale native bindings instead of dereferencing
      invalid pointers; see @ref jni_dynamic_import_in_java for binding lifetime requirements.
    - Fixed leaks of callback return values and temporary class wrappers, and ensured that outstanding
      native wrappers are released during JVM shutdown. Cleanup preserves errors for explicit callers
      without propagating destructor exceptions through JNI; see
      @ref jni_qore_object_lifecycle_managementguide "object lifecycle management".
    - Fixed hangs and aborts during JVM-owned process shutdown, dynamic-class teardown, and explicit
      %Qore process exit, including the macOS shutdown failure.
    - Fixed the JVM failing to start (a crash on Linux) when \c QORE_JNI_JVM_ARGS was set to an empty value or
      contained repeated, leading or trailing spaces.
    - Fixed the process aborting when a Program's Java context could not be created, for example because
      a Java class of the module's dynamic API failed to initialize; loading the module, a
      <tt>%module-cmd(jni)</tt> command, or a \c jni import now fails with the Java exception instead.
    - A Program that is being deleted no longer gets a Java context: creating one there fails with an
      exception instead of changing the Program.

    @par JDBC and data providers

    - Fixed pooled JDBC reads retaining transactions, cursors retaining invalid local JNI references,
      and incomplete statement cleanup. Fixed batch update counts, empty batches, and statement reuse
      after failed binds. Unknown batch counts return \c -1; overflow raises \c JDBC-BATCH-COUNT-ERROR.
      See @ref jdbc_transaction_boundaries "JDBC transaction boundaries".
    - Fixed the \c jdbc driver not executing a statement after reconnecting a lost connection: a write returned
      \c -1 without being executed, and a query returned \c -1 instead of its rows.  The statement is now executed
      again only if it cannot have taken effect (it started a transaction, which the server rolls back); a statement
      in auto-commit mode is never executed twice, and the error is raised instead.  A connection found lost when a
      transaction is started, before the statement is sent (as with the SQL Server driver), is re-established, and
      the statement is executed on the new connection.  A lost transaction raises
      \c JDBC-TRANSACTION-ERROR (it was \c ODBC-TRANSACTION-ERROR), and a \c SQLStatement released by a lost
      connection is prepared again when it is next executed.  See @ref jdbc_lost_connections "lost connections".
    - Fixed the \c jdbc driver ignoring every result after the first: for SQL returning several results, such as a
      batch of statements or a procedure returning several result sets, later result sets and update counts were
      lost, and so were the errors of later statements when the JDBC driver reports them only when their result is
      reached (as the SQL Server driver does).  \c exec(), \c execRaw() and \c select() now return every result,
      in the format of the \c freetds driver; \c selectRow(), \c selectRows(), \c selectColumnar() and
      \c SQLStatement skip update counts and raise an exception for more than one result set; and
      \c SQLStatement::affectedRows() returns the total of the update counts.  A statement with a single result
      returns it as before.  See @ref jdbc_multiple_results "statements with several results".
    - Fixed @ref Qore::SQL::SQLStatement::describe() "SQLStatement::describe()" returning nothing with the \c jdbc
      driver: it describes the columns of the result set as the other DBI drivers do (\c name, \c type, \c maxsize,
      \c native_type, \c internal_id, \c nullable, and \c precision and \c scale for decimals), with \c type
      giving the type of the values returned; the last result set is described after its last row, and SQL without
      a result set is described with an empty hash.  Code that describes statements, such as \c TableMapper's
      statement mappers, gets the columns of \c jdbc statements.  See
      @ref jdbc_statement_describe "describing statement results".
    - Fixed the \c jdbc driver naming a duplicate column of a result with the column's original name and a suffix,
      so that its key could differ in case from the others (ex: \c id and \c ID_1 for the columns \c Id and
      \c ID); a duplicate is named with the lower-case name and a suffix, as every column is (\c id and \c id_1).
    - Fixed @ref exceldataproviderintro "ExcelDataProvider" reading without header options returning
      no data and rejecting cell ranges past column \c Z.
    - Fixed @ref mqttdataproviderintro "MqttDataProvider" failing to load because the required Paho
      MQTTv5 common JAR was missing.

    @par Build and documentation

    - Fixed tracking of generated Java inner-class dependencies in CMake builds.
    - Corrected Java wrapper and provider API links, parameter descriptions, and JMS tables.
      Documentation builds can reject unresolved references with <tt>-DQORE_JNI_STRICT_DOCS=ON</tt>.

